verdict.fix:
1) Make the pairing key-based and consuming, in BOTH endpoints. The one discriminator both sides always carry is the width: the JSON entry's `fabricWidth` is set straight from `opt.cutableWidth` (CostSheetForm.tsx:892-898) and the CAD row has `cutableWidth`. In `updateCostSheet` replace styleCosting.controller.ts:1068-1070 with a matcher that consumes candidates:

  const pool = fabricDetails.map((jf, i) => ({ jf, i }));
  const used = new Set<number>();
  const take = (pred: (jf: FabricDetail) => boolean) => {
    const hit = pool.find(p => !used.has(p.i) && pred(p.jf));
    if (hit) used.add(hit.i);
    return hit?.jf;
  };
  const w = cad.cutableWidth != null ? Number(cad.cutableWidth) : null;
  const cadName = (cad.fabric?.fabricName || cad.greige?.greigeName || '').toLowerCase();
  const jsonFabric =
    take(jf => !!jf.fabricId && (jf.fabricId === cad.fabricId || jf.fabricId === cad.greigeId) && Number(jf.fabricWidth) === w) ??
    take(jf => !!jf.fabricName && cadName.startsWith(jf.fabricName.toLowerCase().trim()) && Number(jf.fabricWidth) === w) ??
    take(jf => !!jf.fabricId && (jf.fabricId === cad.fabricId || jf.fabricId === cad.greigeId)) ??
    take(() => true);   // last-resort positional, but CONSUMING

Note the `startsWith` — plain equality never matches because greige names carry the construction suffix. Apply the same width discriminator in `createCostSheet` (:300-336): its `jsonFabricByFabricId` Map must be keyed on `fabricId + '|' + fabricWidth`, otherwise two approved width options of one fabric collapse to a single JSON entry.

2) Refuse to guess silently. When the last-resort branch fires, or when the deduped CAD-row count and `fabricDetails.length` differ, return a 400/409 naming the fabrics instead of writing a pairing — or at minimum return a `warnings[]` in the response and surface it in CostSheetForm. Today a merchandiser gets "Cost sheet updated successfully" for a rebuilt, crossed plan.

3) Make the read deterministic: `orderBy: [{ updatedAt: 'desc' }, { id: 'asc' }]` at :1055 and :293. STYEW-001's two CAD rows share `updatedAt` to the millisecond, so consecutive saves can currently flip the pairing on identical input.

