Fix in backend/src/services/purchaseOrder.service.ts, updatePurchaseOrder, replacing lines 418-436.

(1) Refuse the edit when anything has been received, mirroring job-work-order.controller.ts:1260-1266's ALREADY_RECEIVED 422 — then DELETE the grn_items.deleteMany at 428-430 outright rather than keeping it. A delete that can only ever destroy an audit row should not exist:
  const existingItems = await tx.purchase_order_items.findMany({ where: { poId: id }, select: { id: true, materialId: true, serviceType: true, receivedQuantity: true } });
  const receipts = await tx.grn_items.count({ where: { poItemId: { in: existingItems.map(i => i.id) } } });
  if (receipts > 0 || existingItems.some(i => Number(i.receivedQuantity) > 0)) {
    throw new Error('Cannot replace items on a purchase order that already has goods received — cancel or short-close it instead.');
  }

(2) Stop destroying the links. Preferred shape — diff instead of replace: match each incoming item to an existing one by materialId (or serviceType+serviceDescription), UPDATE those rows in place so their ids survive (which keeps requirement_po_links, po_source_links, service_requirement_po_links and order_thread_requirements.poItemId intact), create only genuinely new lines, delete only lines the user actually removed. On a quantity change, re-point the surviving links' allocatedQuantity proportionally, the same way mrp.service.ts:3994-4009 allocates.

(3) If a full replace must stay, then capture-and-restore plus revert, mirroring cancelPurchaseOrder:898-910 and job-work-order.controller.ts:1289-1305: read requirement_po_links (requirementId, allocatedQuantity, receivedQuantity) and po_source_links BEFORE the delete; re-create them against the new item ids matched by materialId; and for any requirement whose material no longer appears in the new item set, revert material_requirements `status: { in: ['PO_GENERATED','PO_SENT'] } → 'PO_REQUIRED'` in the same transaction, refusing outright for links with receivedQuantity > 0. Assert the updateMany count and throw on mismatch (precedent: mrp.service.ts:3945-3952) so the revert can never silently no-op.

(4) Do the same for the two paths the finding missed: revert work_order_service_requirements `{ in: ['PO_GENERATED','IN_PROGRESS'] } → 'PENDING', purchaseOrderId: null` for dropped service_requirement_po_links (matching job-work-order.controller.ts:1286), and re-point or revert order_thread_requirements.poItemId (its FK is SET NULL, so those rows strand at PO_GENERATED silently too).

(5) Drop the hardcoded `receivedQuantity: 0` at line 475 in favour of the matched item's existing value — moot once (1) lands, but it removes the trap for whoever next widens editableStatuses.

(6) Guardrail, per the project's smart-hook convention: add a drift-detector flagging any delete/deleteMany on purchase_order_items, requirement_po_links, grn_items or po_source_links inside backend/src/services/** that is not preceded in the same function by a receipt check AND a requirement-status revert — the mechanical signature of this bug class, which scripts/hooks/drift-detectors.js currently has nothing for.
